@{<NOTE>
<M>\newcommand{\v}[1]{\boldsymbol{#1}}</M>
<TITLE>Assignment 1</TITLE>
Each problem carries two marks. Please submit your solutions in handwritten hard copy to your TAs
 on or before Aug 31, 2026.    

<EXR>Let <M>(X,Y)</M>  be jointly distributed with density
<D>f(x,y) = <CASES>x+y <IF>x,y\in[0,1]</IF> 0<ELSE/></CASES>.</D>
Find the density of <M>(X^2+Y, Y).</M>  Do not forget to mention the support.
</EXR>

<EXR>
If <M>X_1,...,X_{10}</M>  are iid <M>N(\mu, \sigma^2)</M>  where <M>mm\in\rr</M>  and
 <M>\sigma^2>0</M>  are both unknown. Find <M>a > 0</M>  such that <M>P(*(-a \leq [[\hat
 \mu-\mu][\hat \sigma]] \leq a)*) = 0.95,</M>  where <M>\hat \mu = \bar X</M>  and <M>\hat \sigma^2 = [[1][9]]\sum_1^{10}(X_i-\bar X)^2.</M>  
Your answer should be in terms of <M>F_k(\cdot),</M>  the distribution function of the <M>t_{(k)}</M>  distribution.
</EXR>

<EXR>A branching process has progeny distribution 
<D>P(X=k) = <CASES>[[15]]<IF>k=0,1,2,3,4</IF> 0<ELSE/></CASES>.</D>
Show that the extinction probability is <M> < 1.</M>
</EXR>

<EXR>If <M>\v X</M>  has <M>N_3(\v \mu, \Sigma)</M>  distribution, where <M>\mu= <MAT>1\\2\\3</MAT></M>  and
<D>\Sigma = <MAT>1 & 0 & 0\\0 & 2 & 0\\0 & 0 & 3</MAT>,</D>
then prove or disprove the statement:
<Q><M>X_1+X_2+X_3</M>  and <M>X_1-2X_2+X_3</M>  are independent.</Q>
 </EXR>

<EXR>Let <M>X</M>  be any nonnegative random variable with finite third moment (i.e.,
 <M>E(X^3)</M>  is a). Then show that
<D>\forall \epsilon>0~~P(X\geq \epsilon) \leq \frac{E(X^3)}{\epsilon^3}.</D>
</EXR>
</NOTE>@} 
